@brief
A ticket.

    PAY-812  The legacy gateway was switched off on Friday. Delete what
             it left behind and make the migration file runnable.

Four buffers. `:bn` and `:bp` move between them.

  1. payment.service.ts — `legacySettle` is dead with the gateway. The
     method goes, and so does the blank line above it, so the file reads
     as though it had never been there.

  2. errors.json — the two `PAYMENT_LEGACY_*` codes are retired. They sit
     next to each other. Both lines go; every other code stays.

  3. migrations.sql — six statements, none of them terminated. Every line
     needs a semicolon on the end. There are no blank lines in the file.

  4. README.md — history. It mentions the legacy gateway because the
     gateway existed. Do not touch it.

Every edit in this ticket can be done with a range and a command, from
wherever the cursor happens to be. Nothing here needs a visual selection
or a count of lines.

@keys
| `:.-1,/^  }/d` | from the line above the cursor to the next two-space `}` |
| `:/pat/;+1d` | the next match and the line under it |
| `:%normal A;` | press `A;` on every line |
| `:bn` `:bp` | next and previous buffer |
| `:wqa` | write everything and quit |

@hints
Do them in buffer order and you never go backwards. The first one is the
mixed range from earlier in the chapter: search to the signature, then
delete from `.-1` to the method's own closing brace.
--
For the two error codes, a comma range would resolve `+1` against the
cursor, which is still on line 1. A semicolon resolves it against the
match. That is the difference the two separators exist for.
--
The SQL file is one `:%normal`. The README needs nothing at all — just
`:wqa` at the end, which leaves an unmodified buffer alone.
--
`/legacySettle` Enter, `:.-1,/^  }/d` Enter, `:bn`, `:/LEGACY/;+1d` Enter,
`:bn`, `:%normal A;` Enter, `:wqa` Enter.

@solution
    /legacySettle<Enter>  :.-1,/^  }/d<Enter>
    :bn<Enter>            :/LEGACY/;+1d<Enter>
    :bn<Enter>            :%normal A;<Enter>
    :wqa<Enter>

Sixty-six keystrokes for three files, and the cursor was on line 1 of
two of them the whole time.

The `;` in `:/LEGACY/;+1d` is the piece most people get wrong first.
Written with a comma, `+1` means "one below the cursor" — line 2 — and
the range comes out backwards, so vim asks whether to swap it. The
semicolon moves the cursor to the first address before working out the
second, which is what "the match and the line after it" actually means.
Comma for two independent addresses, semicolon when the second one is
relative to the first.

`:%normal A;` on the SQL is the whole reason to know `:normal`. The
alternatives are a macro — record, then `6@q`, and count the lines — or
`:%s/$/;/`, which is fine here and stops being fine the moment the edit
is not expressible as a substitution. `:normal` has no such limit: if you
can do it to one line with the keyboard, you can do it to a range.

The README is the part of the ticket that is graded and has no keys. Most
of what a cleanup touches is not what the cleanup is about, and knowing
which buffer to leave alone is a skill like any other. `:wqa` writes only
what changed, so an untouched buffer stays untouched.

@start payment.service.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { paymentGateway } from '../gateways';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async settle(paymentId: string) {
    const result = await paymentGateway.settle(paymentId);

    if (!result.ok) {
      throw exception('PAYMENT_SETTLEMENT_FAILED');
    }

    return result;
  }

  public async legacySettle(paymentId: string) {
    const result = await paymentGateway.settleLegacy(paymentId);

    return result;
  }

  public async status() {
    return paymentGateway.status();
  }
}

@expected payment.service.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { paymentGateway } from '../gateways';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async settle(paymentId: string) {
    const result = await paymentGateway.settle(paymentId);

    if (!result.ok) {
      throw exception('PAYMENT_SETTLEMENT_FAILED');
    }

    return result;
  }

  public async status() {
    return paymentGateway.status();
  }
}

